The attached image (Figure 10 from Lu et al., 2015) illustrates the local inflammatory response and cellular uptake of Nano-11 with antigen (OVA) in mouse skeletal muscle 24 hours post-intramuscular injection. The top row, representing Nano-11 + OVA, shows significant recruitment of Mac-2+ monocytes/macrophages and MHCII+ cells, which are shown to contain intracellular Nano-11. In contrast, the bottom row, representing soluble OVA only, demonstrates minimal inflammatory cell infiltration and no Nano-11 uptake, highlighting Nano-11's role in recruiting antigen-presenting cells and facilitating antigen delivery.

The attached image can significantly contribute to applications in cancer research by demonstrating a mechanism for targeted delivery and activation of antigen-presenting cells (APCs) *in situ*. This visualized recruitment and uptake of nanoparticles by monocytes/macrophages and MHCII+ cells could be leveraged in cancer vaccine development. By adsorbing tumor-associated antigens to Nano-11, this system could efficiently deliver antigens to APCs at a tumor-proximal injection site, facilitating robust anti-tumor T cell responses crucial for cancer immunotherapy.